.TH AXINETD 1 "15 September 2026" "axcall" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
axinetd \- run a program for inbound AX.25 calls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B axinetd
.RI [ options ]
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B axinetd
answers inbound AX.25 calls. Each call either runs a program with the session
on its standard input and output, or is forwarded to a local TCP service.
.PP
That first form is the point of it. Writing a packet service becomes writing a
program that reads standard input and writes standard output, in any language,
with no library to link and no protocol to learn:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import sys
print("Hello from a three line BBS.\\r", end="", flush=True)
for line in sys.stdin:
    print(f"You said: {line.strip()}\\r", end="", flush=True)
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is what
.B ax25d
did, without the kernel AX.25 stack it depended on.
.B axinetd
carries its own AX.25 v2.2 data link and speaks KISS to a TNC over a serial
port or over TCP, exactly as
.BR axcall (1)
does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I port
The AX.25 port to answer on: a name from the ports file, a device path, or
.IR host : port
for a KISS-over-TCP modem. See
.BR axcall (1)
for the ports file.
.TP
.BI \-c " file" "\fR, \fP" \-\-config " file"
Read this rules file instead of searching the usual paths.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial " device" [: baud ]
.TQ
.BI \-\-tcp " host" : port
Name the transport directly instead of giving a
.IR port .
.TP
.BI \-\-baud " rate"
Serial baud rate. Overrides a rate from the ports file. The default is 57600.
.TP
.BI \-p " bytes" "\fR, \fP" \-\-paclen " bytes"
Maximum information field per frame (N1), 1 to 256.
.TP
.BI \-w " frames" "\fR, \fP" \-\-window " frames"
Send window (k), 1 to 7, or 1 to 63 with
.BR \-\-mod128 .
.TP
.B \-\-mod128
Prefer AX.25 v2.2 (SABME, modulo 128).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-xid
Skip the pre-connect XID exchange that negotiates selective reject.
.TP
.BI \-\-keepalive " seconds"
T3, the inactive-link timer. The default is 300.
.TP
.BI \-\-txdelay " ms"
.TQ
.BI \-\-persist " 0-255"
.TQ
.BI \-\-slottime " ms"
.TQ
.BI \-\-txtail " ms"
KISS channel-access parameters, as
.BR kissparms (8)
set them per interface. Timers are in milliseconds and must be a multiple of
10, the KISS unit.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-root
Run as root anyway. See SECURITY.
.TP
.B \-d
Trace every frame, sent and received, to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Log errors only, rather than a line per call.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version, and the versions of the libraries built in.
.SH RULES FILE
.RS
.nf
# callsign   port    action
M0LTE\-1      radio   exec  /usr/local/bin/bbs \-\-user %r
M0LTE\-2      radio   tcp   127.0.0.1:8080
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read from
.I /etc/axcall/inetd
and then
.IR ~/.config/axcall/inetd ,
the second replacing same-callsign entries in the first, or from the single
file named by
.BR \-c " or " $AXCALL_INETD .
.PP
The callsign called is the whole address, so it is also the whole routing
decision. There are no port numbers on the AX.25 side; give each service its
own SSID.
.PP
The port column names a port from the ports file and says which radio the rule
is for. A
.RB ` \- '
means whichever port this instance was started on. Rules for other ports are
ignored, so one file can describe every service on a machine.
.TP
.B exec
Runs a program with the session on its standard input and output. The path must
be absolute: a bare name would be resolved against whatever
.B PATH
the service inherited, which is not a thing to leave to chance when the trigger
is a stranger calling in.
.IP
.B %r
in an argument is replaced with the calling station, which is also passed as
.B AX25_CALLER
in the environment.
.IP
Arguments are split on whitespace and there is no quoting. An argument needing
a space wants a wrapper script, which is clearer than a config line that has
grown its own shell.
.IP
The program's standard error goes to the log, not down the link. Sending it to
the caller would leak paths and stack traces to a stranger and interleave them
with whatever the program meant to say.
.IP
When the link closes, the program's standard input is closed. A program that
has not exited ten seconds later is killed, because an inbound call must not be
able to leave a process behind.
.TP
.B tcp
Hands the call to a local service instead. The service gets a byte stream and
no idea who is on the other end.
.SH SECURITY
.B axinetd
runs programs chosen by a config file whenever a stranger calls in, and it has
no way to drop privileges before doing so. As root, one careless line is a root
shell for anyone with a radio, so it refuses to start as root unless
.B \-\-allow\-root
is given. Run it as a dedicated unprivileged user.
.PP
There is no authentication in AX.25. A callsign in a received frame is a claim,
not a credential, and anyone can send any callsign they like. Treat
.B %r
and
.B AX25_CALLER
as a hint about who is calling, never as proof.
.PP
One call at a time per station. A second call from a station already in session
is ignored rather than given a second program, because both would be writing
down one link.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped cleanly.
.TP
.B 1
Something unexpected went wrong.
.TP
.B 2
The command line or the rules file was wrong, or there was nothing to answer
for on this port.
.TP
.B 3
The modem could not be opened.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/inetd
System rules file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/axcall/inetd
Per-user rules file, read second.
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
System ports file, shared with
.BR axcall (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXCALL_INETD
If set, the only rules file consulted.
.TP
.B AXCALL_PORTS
If set, the only ports file consulted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR axcall (1),
.BR axsocks (1)
.PP
